  "topic_id": "grey-minimum-landing-zone",
  "title": "Minimum Landing Zone",
  "markdown": "# Minimum Landing Zone\n\n![Visual reference from Minimum Landing Zone](../../assets/figures/page-0158-visual.webp)\n\nWind Direction\n\nLZ\n\nApproach\n\n### Aircraft Arrival\n\n* Identify Scene and LZ Incident Command\n* Establish radio communications prior to landing\n* State Fire or State EMS are the default radio frequencies\n* Advise pilot of terrain conditions, vertical obstructions, and wind direction\n* Secure LZ and appoint personnel to guard main and tail rotors (i.e., Rotor Officer) as per current ground safety course\n* Notify pilot if patient is packaged and ready for hot load\n\n### Operating Around Helicopter\n\n* Approach aircraft with crew escort only\n* Approach aircraft 90 degrees to door only\n* Avoid tail boom and rotor at all times\n* Eye and ear protection should be worn\n* Do not carry anything above shoulder height\n* Secure all loose medical and personnel equipment\n* Spotlights, headlights, and/or hand-held lights should not be pointed directly at the helicopter\n",
  "plain_text": "Minimum Landing Zone Wind Direction LZ Approach Aircraft Arrival Identify Scene and LZ Incident Command Establish radio communications prior to landing State Fire or State EMS are the default radio frequencies Advise pilot of terrain conditions, vertical obstructions, and wind direction Secure LZ and appoint personnel to guard main and tail rotors (i.e., Rotor Officer) as per current ground safety course Notify pilot if patient is packaged and ready for hot load Operating Around Helicopter Approach aircraft with crew escort only Approach aircraft 90 degrees to door only Avoid tail boom and rotor at all times Eye and ear protection should be worn Do not carry anything above shoulder height Secure all loose medical and personnel equipment Spotlights, headlights, and/or hand-held lights should not be pointed directly at the helicopter",
